- How would you adapt this for approximate distinct in extremely high-throughput streams?
- What happens if events arrive in irregular batch sizes or with possible duplicates within a batch?

## Why This Is Asked

This question blends sliding-window technique with binary search to analyze a threshold problem, testing practical algorithm design and reasoning about complexity.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window for fixed-length subarray sums
- Binary search over the answer space
- Time/space trade-offs and edge-case handling
- Practical optimizations (early exit, optional prefix sums)

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxLWithSumAtMost(A, T) {
  const n = A.length;
  const ok = (L) => {
    let sum = 0;
    for (let i = 0; i < L; i++) sum += A[i];
    if (sum <= T) return true;
    for (let i = L; i < n; i++) {
      sum += A[i] - A[i - L];
      if (sum <= T) return true;
    }
    return false;
  };
  let lo = 1, hi = n, ans = 0;
  while (lo <= hi) {
    const mid = (lo + hi) >> 1;
    if (ok(mid)) { ans = mid; lo = mid + 1; } else { hi = mid - 1; }
  }
  return ans;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would the solution change if elements could be negative?
- What are the memory/speed trade-offs if you precompute prefix sums?
- How would you adapt for multiple thresholds T1..Tk in parallel?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests dynamic skyline maintenance with batch updates, including amortized analysis and practical data-structure design.

## Key Concepts
- **2D dominance and Pareto frontier**: A point p dominates q if p.x ≥ q.x and p.y ≥ q.y with at least one strict inequality.
- **Invariant**: Frontier F is sorted by x with strictly increasing y, ensuring no two frontier points dominate each other.
- **Amortized analysis**: Each point is inserted once and removed at most once over n total points.
- **Space**: Uses O(n) space for all historical points plus O(|F|) for the current frontier.

## Data Structure
Use a balanced binary search tree (e.g., TreeMap) keyed by x-coordinate, with y-values stored alongside. This enables O(log |F|) insertion, O(1) access to the rightmost (largest x) point, and O(|F|) in-order traversal.

## Algorithm
1. Sort incoming batch by x-coordinate (O(B log B)).
2. Merge with frontier: for each batch point p, if p.y > current max frontier y, insert p and prune any frontier points with y ≤ p.y.
3. Enumerate frontier via in-order traversal.

## Code Example
```javascript
class Skyline {
  constructor() { this.frontier = new Map(); }

  insertBatch(points) {
    const sorted = points.sort((a, b) => a.x - b.x);
    let maxY = 0;

    for (const p of sorted) {
      if (p.y <= maxY) continue;

      for (const [x, y] of this.frontier) {
        if (y <= p.y) this.frontier.delete(x);
      }

      this.frontier.set(p.x, p.y);
      maxY = p.y;
    }
  }

  getFrontier() {
    return Array.from(this.frontier.entries())
      .sort((a, b) => a[0] - b[0]);
  }
}
```

## Complexity Summary
| Operation | Cost |
|-----------|------|
| Batch insertion | O(B log B + |F|) amortized |
| Enumerate frontier | O(|F|) |
| Space | O(n) |

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of amortized analysis with a practical growth strategy, highlighting how linear growth increments behave differently from exponential doubling and how memory copies interact with cache locality.

## Key Concepts
- Amortized cost under linear growth
- Block-based capacity increments (capacity increases by B, not a multiplicative factor)
- Cache locality during bulk copies
- Trade-offs between growth frequency and per-copy work

## Code Example
```javascript
class Queue {
  constructor(capacity = B) {
    this.buffer = new Array(capacity);
    this.head = 0;
    this.tail = 0;
    this.size = 0;
  }

  enqueue(item) {
    if (this.size === this.buffer.length) {
      const newBuffer = new Array(this.buffer.length + B);
      for (let i = 0; i < this.size; i++) {
        newBuffer[i] = this.buffer[(this.head + i) % this.buffer.length];
      }
      this.buffer = newBuffer;
      this.head = 0;
      this.tail = this.size;
    }
    this.buffer[this.tail] = item;
    this.tail = (this.tail + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size++;
  }

  dequeue() {
    if (this.size === 0) return undefined;
    const item = this.buffer[this.head];
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.buffer.length;
    this.size--;
    return item;
  }
}
```

## B's Influence
- **Total copies**: Larger B reduces growth frequency, cutting total copy work from O(N²) toward O(N) (since copies ≈ N²/(2B))
- **Cache behavior**: Smaller B keeps working set in cache but triggers more frequent expensive copies; larger B improves sequential access patterns during copies but may evict useful cache contents when reallocating
